In article <1993Jan15.194312.3438@sol.UVic.CA>, zapantis@uvphys.phys.uvic.ca (Nik Zapantis) writes:
|>In the Jan 4th. edition of DN&R there is an interview with W.R. Demmer 
|>DEC VP for Alpha and VAX systems. 
|>There (p. 42), he states that there is no schedule for the release of 
|>a 64-bit OpenVMS version and he goes on to state a number of reasons for the
|>delays.
|>Does anyone know or care to comment (DEC?) as to why a 64-bit VMS is not
|>a priority? Is it only the address space that is not 64-bits, or the entire
|>OS?
|>Does anyone know what kind of a performance penalty we'll pay for not 
|>running a (full?) 64-bit OS in a number crunching environment?

1. The current limitation is that a single process can not exceed 1 gigabyte
  of P0 and 1 gigabyte of P1 space.  Removing this restriction requires changing
  any and all data structures that allocate 32 bits for an address pointer.
  There are no firm plans for when new interfaces will be provided with the
  current 32 bit pointers removed.  That is what Bill Demmer is referring to.

2. VMS code that is native to Alpha will use 64 bits for any arithmetic if
   desired.  This is being generated by all the compilers.  The current
   compilers that have the concept of a pointer use 32 bits for the pointer
   for the most part.  Some languages, FORTRAN is an example, do not have
   pointers, so there is no impact.

3. The calling standard used by all native and translated code uses 64 bit
   values for both integers AND ADDRESSES.  The current addresses are 32 bits
   sign extended to 64 bits.  Thus the current calling standard will work
   correctly with, and is infact a full 64 bit calling standard.

4. Most of the exec code has been modified to use 64 bit addresses, but for
   compatibility with translated code and recompiled code with builtin
   assumptions about the size of pointer, system service interfaces must
   assume 32 bit pointers, but these pointers are passed as 32 bits sign
   extended to 64 bits.  Whether there are hidden assumptions in some of
   the exec code is unknown since they are never passed addresses that can't
   be represented in 32 bits.

5. The memory management code can deal with physical address spaces much larger
   than 32 bits; in theory it should handle the maximum for 8KB pages and also
   64KB pages, but without actual hardware that use 64KB pages or that have
   2**55 pages, support can not be verified.

6. As far as performance penalties, I don't believe that there is any what so
   ever, but I might be missing something.  One might argue that limiting
   the virtual address space for processes to 32 bits increases performance
   because there is a reduction in memory use which reduces the pages required
   and the I/O and pages faults etc.  The instruction for setting up a 32 bit
   sign extended address is the same length and same number of cycles as for
   a 64 bit address.

