There are a couple of points that I think need to be emphasized, the
first being that PATCHES ARE AVAILABLE ONLINE, they just aren't on the
Internet for FTPing or in newsgroups.  Everyone who is involved in
customer service is absolutely commited to having everything online.

What is at issue within DEC and I'm sure elsewhere is the method of
making the information available.  Certainly there are business issues,
such as who will pay for it, but is really a minor consideration overall.
As an engineer whose job is qualifying VMScluster systems, I have major
reservations about having patches online for purely annoymous FTP.  In
my experience, patches require significant testing, and when combined
with other patches have interesting side effects.  The testing effort
to verify to a marginal level that all possible combinations of patches
have no adverse effects is substantially greater than testing patches
as a unit and applying them as a group.  (The problem is obvious, right?)

There are a number of us who believe that we should release entire product
sets as a single whole, ie., VMS+Rdb+ACMS+DECset+FORTRAN+COBOL+....
Every quarter or every six months, the current releases would be reviewed
for quality and interoperability and the product set would be ECO'd.
(This is infact what DEC's internal MIS organization does.)  The combination
would be tested as best as we can within a limited time, of perhaps three
months and then released.

The problem is that this does not respond well to the dynamics and pace of
the current computer market.  So, that is certainly not going to be the
norm for software releases.  However, you will find increased bundling
taking place over time, in fact, NAS software packages are an example.
When a dozen different components are highly dependent on each other,
someone needs to do the integration up front.

So, what about the problems that slip thru the testing and really cause
problems for a customer?  Clearly, a method of distributing patches is
required.  However, the generality of the patch and the degree of review
and testing must be taken into account before you decide how widely you
distribute the patch.

Let's say for example, you have an application that you purchased from
a third party that won't work with the latest version of DECwindows.
You call the support center, they investigate the problem and determine
that there is an error in the application which caused no problems in
the previous release of DECwindows, but the new release no rejects the
parameters to certain calls.  In order to get you up and running, the
parameter check is patched out.  This patch will then go into a database
with keywords that will lead to this patch whenever the same third
party app is involved or when the symptoms are the same.

Question: should this patch be distributed widely?
Question: do you think the patch is appropriate?
Question: since the incompatibility originated from MIT/OSF, should
          DEC permenantly disable this `feature', support only MIT/OSF's
          solution, blame the third party, say `not supported'...?
Question: when a new solution to the problem arises, should DEC contact
          those who have installed the patch or is it sufficient to
          to depend on the customer tracking the status on an ongoing
          basis even tho he no longer appears to have a problem?

(The example above is partially real, but really fictious.)

I won't argue that DEC's online services, and there are a number of them,
are ideal by the standards I'm suggesting, or even that they meet them
better than the service offered by anyone else.  (Clearly the folklore
suggests that a number of DEC's smaller competitors are much better at
customer support than we are for our competing products.)  However, we
do offer support that touches on the above.  It is possible in some
locales to dialin and query a database for either specific problems or
for possible problems.  It is possible to have know patches downloaded.
And when these patches are superceded, it is possible to update you.
And it is possible to have patched developed by the support center
specifically for your peculiar circumstances.

DEC is also available via major online services (Compuserv, etc) for
specialized product support.  And DEC has and is adding new online
systems systems.  In Europe there is a project that is collecting
reliability data from customer systems and actively working to
identify critical problems, and one of the methods for collecting
the data is the Internet, along with any public mail handling network.

Again, I won't claim that DEC is the leader in this area, that the
policies today are the best ones, or that the policies tomorrow or
the day after will be.  The points I want to make clearly are
 1. DEC is not ignoring the issue
 2. the problem is not as simple as putting patches on gatekeeper
 3. the major issues are not lawyers or management
mjr has argued strongly for placing patches on the Internet, but the
question that I haven't seen him answer is who will be responsible
for the problems that ensue, and I don't mean lawsuits, etc.  When
a manager says `what happens when something goes wrong?', someone
needs to provide an answer, and no one provides an answer then it
is reasonable for the manager to say `work it out and come back to me'.
It isn't reasonable to take patches and just heave them out on the net.

Sorry this is so long, but this is a subject on which I have spent many
years.

mulp
DEC CVG - cluster buster (but I can find bugs in any system)


