Actually, the PRO-350 wasn't years too late.  It really wasn't.  The REAL
problem was the one mentioned in previous postings.  DEC tried to segment
the market place by making the Professional Series end-user only (at least
at first).  You could not do development on the machine itself.  This was
not because of any hardware limitations.  It was because they did not
provide a DCL (or even MCR) interface to the underlying RSX system.  If they
had simply made this a personal RSX system with an added menu system, they
would have had a big winner.  The technology was way ahead of the competition.

And, if the hardware sales had been adequate, maybe some of the innovative
things they tried with software might have worked, too  -- things like
the Digitial Classified Software program or some such.  Who knows?!  As it
is, I have copies of two of the AIS products of those days, EasyEntry and
AIS-PL/I, attractively packaged by DEC with the DCS Seal of Approval, which
serve as reminders of the perils of third-party partnerships with any 
vendor.  (I also have reminders of failed projects with other vendors.)

BTW, by the end of its life, the development restrictions were lifted (too
late) and the PRO-380 made a respectable development system.  But, by then
the world was in love with Intel PCs.  Alas.


