> would have had a big winner.  The technology was way ahead of the competition.

Umm.  Maybe.  But DEC had more going against them on the PRO than the
technical issues you mentioned (although granted that they were motivated
by a mistaken marketing strategy).  Take the Rainbow, for example, which
was introduced at the same time as the PRO.  That was a PC-like machine
with an 8088 processor, and was actually superior to the PC in many
ways (the main way it wasn't was that it had fixed-function slots for
peripherals, so you couldn't build odd boards and plug them in;  but
at that time this was interesting mostly for scientific and engineering
laboratory users who were building their own boards anyway:  things like
FAX boards and all that hadn't been thought of yet).  The Rainbow was
even semi-PC-compatible in that it could run many of the PC programs of
the day (not all, it wasn't BIOS or hardware compatible, but much of the
software of the day wasn't written that close to the hardware because the
IBM-PC was not yet the de facto standard it is today), so it had a fairly
wide selection of software for the time.

DEC was rather pigheaded about trying to push CPM-86/80 on it even after
it had become apparent that the 80x86 world was moving to MS-DOS, but
the main downfall of the Rainbow was the DEC marketing department.  They
did not then, and may not now, know how to sell to non-technical end-users;
nor did they understand how to work with VARs and PC dealers in that low-
end marketplace.  They assumed, incorrectly, that it would be a mirror
image of the higher-end PDP-11 VAR/dealer network, which could accept
lower margins on hardware because much of their profit was in system and
software integration and customization. In the PC market at the time, this
dealer-level pricing strategy was a recipe for death, because it meant
that dealers didn't want to move your product;  they could make more
profit on an IBM.

I have seen no evidence that DEC would have been any less shortsighted
with the PRO even if they had packaged it more attractively.

An embittered former Rainbow developer,

Bruce C. Wright


