 Greetings, netvmsgurus! We are currently attempting to port some code that
uses object libraries onto an alpha running OpenVMS AXP V1.0 (from vms 5.5-1)
demo machine, for benchmarking before making a purchase decision. We have
discovered that the linker works differently on the Alpha than on the 
vax, with respect to object libraries and symbol resolution. Specifically,
the following code and commands link cleanly on a VAX running VMS 5.5-1,
but fails miserably on the alpha. Error msgs follow the code and commands
used:

Main routine (MAIN.FOR)
	call sub1
	end

File containing subroutines (MODULE.FOR)
	subroutine sub1
	print *,'this is sub1'
	return
	end
c
	subroutine sub2
	call sub3
	return
	end

Compile commands:
$ FORTRAN MAIN
$ FORTRAN MODULE

Library command:
$ LIB/CREATE MODLIB MODULE

Link command:
$ LINK MAIN,MODLIB/LIB

Alpha link error output:
%LINK-W-NUDFSYMS, 1 undefined symbol:
%LINK-I-UDFSYM,         SUB3
%LINK-W-USEUNDEF, undefined symbol SUB3 referenced
        in psect $LINK$ offset %X00000020
        in module SUB1 file DISK$USER:[PHYSICS]MODLIB.OLB;1

The alpha is including SUBROUTINE SUB2 in the executable, even though 
it is never called, and thus causing excessive confusing linker output. 
The executable *DOES* work, but we would like to find a way to modify this
linker behavior. It looks as though the qualifier /SELECTIVE_SEARCH 
on the LIBRARIAN command would do it, but no permutations I have tried 
with this qualifier have eliminated the error. Can anyone out there 
advise me as to correct usage of this qualifier to eliminate these error
msgs? They make debugging a link of a large piece of software sheer h*ll, 
as we have ended  up with as many as 93 undefined symbols, when possibly 
only a very few are really needed or valid.

Also, can anyone point me to some documentation that highlights this change
in linker behavior from VAX VMS to the Alpha VMS? I've been searching
through bookreader docs for the Alpha (a painful exercise in itself, but
we won't get into that %;^) and find the behavior is documented, but not
highlighted as being something different from current versions of VAX VMS,
nor as something to watch out for. 

Please respond via email (as well as posting if you feel this is of 
general interest) as I tend to be behind in this newsgroup. Thanks very
much for any help you can offer!!!

Judith Reed
jareed@mailbox.syr.edu
Sysmgr, Syracuse Univ. High Energy Physics 



