I posted the note/question about a problem with a Tahiti-II drive a
few days ago.  Well, I think I have a handle on it.  I was speaking with Randy
Douglas, sales representative for Mesa Integrated Systems (800-487-7911), about
another topic, and I mentioned the Tahiti-II problem.  Turns out he knew all
about it.  There is, I'm told, a problem/bug with the DEC software that
controls SCSI devices, which causes a timeout when used with optical drives. 
I'm unclear if this is a system firmware or a VMS device driver problem, but I
suspect it is a driver problem, since one of the proposed solutions (see below)
is a software one. Randy outlined two solutions for me.

The software solution requires substituting another device driver for DEC's
DKDRIVER.  It is low cost, only $465 for Universities (your price may vary),
and can be used on sevaral drives.  Once DEC comes out with revised software
this won't be needed, but I'm not holding my breath on that one.  Its only
drawbacks are that it may require changing when you upgrade operating systems,
and you probably can't call DEC for help if there are any problems.

The hardware solution is the purchase of a CMD SKEA board for each drive to be
adapted.  The SKEA (pronounced "ski") board would sit between the Tahiti-II
drive and the SCSI bus and somehow compensate for the irregularity.  The board
is fairly small and can likely be configured within the optical drive's
enclosure.  This would cost $650 per drive; again, this is University pricing. 
The advantage is that this is independent of the system the drive is connected
to, and is independent of operating system revision. 

I'm probably going to go with the software solution.  It seems cleaner, and
we're not likely to be moving the drives about.

Another note: Since this is (probably) a driver problem, people with other
optical drives, not just the Tahiti-II, may be experiencing similar
difficulties, and could probably benefit from these solutions.

