Thanks to all who responded to this question. It seems fairly certain that the
problem is not with the librarian or linker, but with the FORTRAN compiler
on the ALPHA, which is DEC FORTRAN as opposed to VAX FORTRAN. The best
explanation I received was as follows:
******************************************************************************
This isn't the linker or the librarian, it's the compiler.  On VAX, the
Fortran compiler puts out a separate object module definition for each
routine; to the librarian, linker, it looks as if each routine is in its
own module.  On AXP, the compiler, by default, compiles all the routines in
a source file into one module.  This is why when one routine is extracted, the
other comes with it.

If you want the VAX behavior, compile with the /SEPARATE_COMPILATION
qualifier.  See the DEC Fortran for OpenVMS AXP User Manual for more details.
*******************************************************************************

The /SEPARATE_COMPILATION qualifier used during compilation does indeed work.

Another respondent noted the following, which may be of interest to others
working with an ALPHA:
********************************************************************************

BTW, there *ARE* some differences in the linker, especially with regards to
sharable images.  For example, there are no longer transfer vectors written
in Macro, there are symbol vectors specified in the link options file instead.
The biggest change is that you need to specify each sharable image that you
are dependent on explicitly.  For example if you are linking image a which
calls both image b and c, and in addition, b calls c, on VAX, you only need
to specify b when linking a.  On Alpha, you need to say both b and c.  This
resolves a lot of nasty problems that can appear in VAX.
********************************************************************************

Once again, thanks to those who responded!

Judith Reed
jareed@mailbox.syr.edu



