>Howdy, DEC experts.  Does anyone out there actually make use of the CDA
>stuff that comes with Ultrix?  Supposedly, there is a converter/viewer
>for .tif files in the base system, but I can't get it to work on an
>Ultrix 4.2 system.  Specifically, the customer has several .tif files
>they would like to view.  After trying dxvdoc on them and getting the
>message "CDA unsupported format", I tried something really basic:
>
>cdoc -s text -d dtif -o .login.tif .login
>
>which resulted in the following message:
>
>cdoc: Domain converter not found (28609154)
>
>TFM makes this profound observation:
>
> DCVNOTFND: Domain converter not found
>       Level:  Error
>      Explanation: The required domain converter could not be found.
>
>BTW, I can run the cdoc command on a text file and successfully convert
>it to DEC's DDIF format and use the dxvdoc to look at it, so *part* of
>the CDA stuff works.  I'm either missing some piece of the system, or
>it's broke down for .tif files.  Does anyone know?
>
>----Dale Cook
>

Hi,

The reason why you get this message is that the converter for TIF is not
installed. 
CDA is a compound document architecture (text,vector graphics,images,...).
The interchange format is DDIF. In the architecture there is the notion
of front end and back end converters ( from XXX to DDIF and from DDIF to YYY).
There exist an optional product, which is a library of different convertors
(front and back end). Check with your sales rep if a TIF converter exists
within this product ( called CDA Converter Library ), I don't know and don't
have the product spec with me here. If it does not exist, you can use
the CDA programming library ( included with Ultrix ) to write a converter
(which isn't too difficult since it is just converting an image, not
a complete compound document).


Hope this helps.

Dominique


