  The Ultrix CAM SCSI interface will take advantage of an ANSI SCSI-2 compliant
capability for synchronous transfer. That is to say, if the target device (for
your case, read "disk drive") is capable of negotiating synchronous transfer
per the specification. However, a drive which has it's synchronous capability
set by a jumper (which I wouldn't call a negotiable setting) won't work.


>>Does the 5000/240 support Fast SCSI-2 or just SCSI-2?


  The DS5000/240 supports a standard clock rate ANSI SCSI-2 implementation. If
you wait about a week there will be a new version of the TRI/ADD Shippable
Product Catalog available on gatekeeper. This will include a couple of new
TURBOchannel options (from 3rd party vendors) that will support FAST SCSI-2.

You might want to keep in mind that without some improvements on the controller
you won't necessarily see all of the speed you think you will (this is true for
anybody) as the disk mechanics don't change on a lot of these devices. As a
result of this, you'll see a goof improvement if the data is in the cache of
the SCSI controller on the disk. However, your seek time on the platter is not
likely to improve (barring a RAID style controller). Just a caveat.


