>	library, a calculator/spreadsheet thing that used 64 bits. Is that
>	still available? It was a SIMULATED 64 bits if I remember, could
>	it have been made REAL in the Alpha VMS?

Read Larry's reply again. It is the OS functions (like address space and the
like) which are not "64-bit clean". The math operators certainly are, ie the
H-float functions do all 64 bits in one go. There is no performance "penalty" 
from the limited address space (only a brick wall if you need more :-). 

(Note: G-float is an extended range but reduced precision D-float. Although
not using the IEEE double precision internal format, it has approximately the
same range and is thus useful when porting programs from the IEEE world. It is
an emulated format on most (all ?) VAX hardware and thus slower than D-float.
I don't know how the Alpha does G-floating, but I don't think it has to emulate
and anyway, you can to IEEE directly on it.)

Wish I could afford an Alpha...

Cheers


