References: <C10tHx.LBI@alsvid.une.edu.au> <1jg36pINNihe@gap.caltech.edu>
Date: Tue, 19 Jan 1993 08:35:56 GMT


The one and only way to force a crash on any VAX is as follows:

>>> D PC FFFFFFFF
>>> D PSL 41F0000
>>> C

Note the "4" in the PSL, This will switch you to the interrupt stack. If you
don't do this, you can have potential problems forcing a crash.

To enable writing the memory contents to disk the following sysgen parameter
must be set:

SYSGEN> DUMPBUG 1

To write the crash dump to disk a couple of options:

o Write the dump to the pagefile (SYS$SYSTEM:PAGEFILE.SYS)
  This is done by default if DUMPBUG is 1. If however you're interested
  in it's contents, you have to set SYSGEN> SAVEDUMP 1. A word of caution
  as this will keep the pagefile occupied untill the dump is released with
  $ ANA/CRASH/RELEASE SYS$SYSTEM:PAGEFILE.SYS

o Write the dump to a seperate dumpfile. If during booting the file 
  SYS$SPECIFIC:[SYSEXE]SYSDUMP.DMP is found it wil use this file to write
  the dump to. 

o SYS$SPECIFIC:[SYSEXE]SYSDUMP.DMP not necessarily has to be a physical file.
  It also can be a link to a common dump file in SYS$COMMON:[SYSEXE]Whatever.DMP
  A technique widely used on clusters to save disk space. A word of caution
  again: When in a cluster more than 1 system goes down, those systems will
  overwrite eachothers dump file..

There is some smoke and mirrors on partial dumping, but 
SYSGEN> HELP PARA DUMPSTYLE tells you all about it as well as BUGCHECKFATAL
to turn non-fatal BUGCHECKS into FATAL, hence a system crash.

/Peter

