The tape normally contains megabytes of data. This takes a while to download.
But most importantly, the tape is customized. IBM knows exactly what software
and layered products I have, and when I asked for the patch I get (in
principle) all the other material I might need. I don't just FTP the one file I
decided to apply and find out that the hard way that a concurrent fix is
needed. The difficulty is not to put the fixes on the Internet, but the actual
programs and databases which decide what to put on the tape and what to print
on the memo. And I'm not even beginning to address the legal/managerial
concerns of having fixes downloaded from a potentially cracked machine through
an insecure network (the tapes are shipped in tamper proof packages which can
probably be faked by someone with the right types of contacts and resources,
but they are at least proof against bored students who have just found out how
to get root privileges).

> My point is, DEC has the stuff online, including the binaries and documents
> that go with the patch.  It would take a single ftp command to put all the
> stuff on gatekeeper.  No number of committees sitting around talking about
> it can get it done, though.  It just takes one single person, and a few minutes
> (at the most) for each patch.

I don't know about DEC, but IBM has tens of thousands of patches just to the
"mainline" products (the ones most customers have). Every day there are new
patches, new procedures, warnings about existing patches, and so on. The reason
is not that the developers can't write code (although there is a bit of that as
well) but simply that when you have hundreds of "mainline" products with 2-3
supported releases each and they interact with each other, problems are bound
to show up no matter how good your programmers are.

  Eric


