In article <1993Jan15.194312.3438@sol.UVic.CA>, zapantis@uvphys.phys.uvic.ca (Nik Zapantis) writes:
> 
> In the Jan 4th. edition of DN&R there is an interview with W.R. Demmer 
> DEC VP for Alpha and VAX systems. 
> There (p. 42), he states that there is no schedule for the release of 
> a 64-bit OpenVMS version and he goes on to state a number of reasons for the
> delays.
> Does anyone know or care to comment (DEC?) as to why a 64-bit VMS is not
> a priority? Is it only the address space that is not 64-bits, or the entire
> OS?

I'm not sure what you mean about asking whether it's just the address
space that's not 64-bits or the whole OS.

If you're asking if the Alpha has a 32-bit mode, the answer is no.  You
can use 64-bit integers on AlphaVMS if your compiler supports it.  It's
just the address space that is not 64 bits wide;  any number crunching
that you do can use the full 64 bits.

> Does anyone know what kind of a performance penalty we'll pay for not 
> running a (full?) 64-bit OS in a number crunching environment?

That depends on whether you need a 64-bit address space, for large
simulations, for example.  Since a 32-bit address space is (just)
adequate for the largest modern systems, I'd guess that the near-term
impact would be fairly small unless you plan on configuring a _really_
large simulation on a huge machine.  Alpha-VMS only runs in the low
order 32 bits of the address space, for compatibility with exising
software that uses 32-bit pointers for memory allocation and OS calls.
That's what DEC is debating about changing.

Bruce C. Wright


