You were hoping for a flame, weren't you? I'd like to understand what makes you
think that a system call that has been there for 10 years (with the
corresponding amount of programs making use of the call) and that is documented
as taking a longword in its second parameter is going to magically start
accepting either a longword or quadword based on what the user meant at the
time he typed the code. DEC is going to have to provide a mechanism to say
whether your parameter lists are 32 or 64 bits, and while it might even be
possible to maintain full source compatibility (you probably wouldn't believe
me if I said IBM had no trouble achieving this), you will have to contend with
applications which haven't been ported yet and that you have to call, and many
other practical problems which have nothing to do with operating system design.
As long as there is one little part in a 200k lines package which doesn't
support the new addressing, the package doesn't support it. The vendor of that
package may only have 5% of customers asking for this change, because others
have no need for more than 4G of addressing space with this package, and may
postpone the decision for a while. This is exactly what DEC has done with VMS,
not due to design problems but simply because they have only so many
programmers and they have more important things to do at the moment. Similarly,
IBM originally decided that there was no urgent need to allow DCL programs to
use up more than 16M for their variables - even though the interpreter had been
carefully written to be 31-bit clean. With the second release and all things
stabilized they fixed the 2-3 system interfaces in the interpreter which were
the source of the limitation. It was probably only a few days of work, but the
decision was based on lack of user need for the change. So how many programs
do you have which can't fit in 4G? :-)

