I don't know about DEC but I know that Apple has an FTP site where they
put lots of interesting stuff.  However the big problem that the guy
maintaining it has is LEGALITIES.  Apple's software licencing people
just don't like giving stuff away for free to anyone.  There's a whole
array of issues involved...

o Does the software contain components that can't be given away (like
  fonts licenced from some other company)?  I imagine this must be
  huge tracts of fun for unix people, where half the code is still
  owned by USL.
o If you make it FTPable then international users can access it.  How
  does this affect the copyright/licencing especially given the
  different laws in each country.
o Is it possible to actually lose control of your software by making
  it so readily available.  No one wants to start a law suit because
  some over-enthusiastic internet user broke their licence.  Law suits
  are *expensive*.
o How do you prevent anyone just getting the software?  This may
  not seem bad for Apple (they need a Mac to run it on right?) but
  with the rise of Mac clones and emulators it's an important issue.
o Most software today is shrinkwrapped and by breaking the package you
  agree to the software licence.  This type of licencing has been tested
  in the courts.  But if it's available for anonymous FTP then you have
  to put a notice on the software saying that by FTPing you are
  agreeing to the licence.  But will the user ever see it (my Mac client
  doesn't show the silly messages unless I ask for it) and will it
  stand up in court?  Again, the legal division just doesn't want the
  hassle of taking something like this to court.

These issues mean that every piece of software that makes it on to
ftp.apple.com has to be individually authorised.  What a hassle!
So it's not just a case of FTPing the tape up to an anonymous area.

Overall it's much easier to force every customer to prove they have
the rights to the software (by giving them a customer number) and
mail it out to them.

But hell, I'm not advocating that view, just explaining it.

