Let's get a bit more specific.  Once your kernel is built with cdfs, you can
mount either UFS or CDFS (ISO 9660) filesystems.  Let's suppose your CD drive
is /dev/rz0c and you have a directory /cdrom to be its mount point.  Then to
mount an ISO 9660 disk:

	mount -t cdfs {options} /dev/rz0c /cdrom

where {options} of interest might be "noversion" and "nodefperm".  If you
want to put this in /etc/fstab, the line there might be

	/dev/rz2c:/cdrom:ro:0:0:cdfs::

