In article <1993Jan20.001847.1807@eco.twg.com> reece@eco.twg.com writes:
>
>The limitiation of I/Os to 64Kb was lifted quite some time ago. The VAX/VMS
>I/O User's Reference Manual: Part 1 for VAX/VMS V4.4 states:
>
>"Non-DSA disk devices can read or write up to 65.535 bytes in a single
> request. DSA devices connected to an HSC50 can transfer up to 4 billion
> bytes in a single request. In all cases, the maximum size of the transfer
> is limited by the number of pages which can be faulted into the process's
> working set and then locked into memory."
>
>The relevant fields of the IRP were expanded to 32 bits during the V3 -> V4
>enhancements.
>

Yes, but so far I have found no support for >16 bit transfers in any VMS
device driver. Check the CDDB$L_MAXBCNT field. I may not have checked the
HSC-50, but certainly the 70's, as well as DSSI and SCSI devices.
It is particularly disappointing that the XMI DSSI controller (forget the code
name) is still stuck at 16 bits, as it is the highest performance controller
from DEC currently. Will be interesting to see what the new XMI SCSI controller
will do. But to sum up, >16 bit disk transfers are in effect a lie: there is
theoretical support only.



