I haven't seen the UNIX Review article you mentioned yet, but my first guess 
is that this is a "way biased" review.

Alpha AXP hardware is very stable (has been for a long time) and is shipping
revenue-quality today.  It is supported by production-quality OpenVMS and an
early ship version of DEC OSF/1.  Read that as field test quality DEC OSF/1. 
The stability of field test DEC OSF/1 on Alpha AXP is good enough that we feel
we can make widely available for anyone developing applications and, in fact,
have an early ship program in place today.  This program will remain in place
until DEC OSF/1 V1.2 starts shipping in March.

All of the feedback I've heard is that the quality is excellent -- especially
for a field test.  I'm know there are some problems that are being addressed 
and work continues on optimization -- but ,in general, most customers seem
pretty pleased.  That is far from Digital field service people scrambling 
in "kernal debug" mode.

In terms of the number of applications available today for DEC OSF/1 on Alpha
AXP - I'm amazed that he thinks the number is as high as 50-100.  After all, 
it *is* still in field test.  There are 1000's of 3rd party providers 
publicaly commited to Alpha AXP ports and the number continues to grow.  
Some are OpenVMS only.  Some are DEC OSF/1 only.  Some are both.  Some are
Windows NT.  Someone will ask, so I'll say right now that somewhere between 
40% to 50% of the commited 3rd party providers are targetting DEC OSF/1.

Most UNIX 3rd party providers are in the process of porting or are in field 
test themselves.  I don't have access to the exact numbers, but do know that 
by the end of this quarter (when DEC OSF/1 is shipping production quality) 
the number should be somewhere between 250-500 and ramping up dramatically 
the following quarters

We have porting centers all over the world and are providing expertise in migration of applications to DEC OSF/1.  From what I've seen, most ports 
take anywhere from 3 hours to 3 weeks.  Of course, once the port is complete
most commercial 3rd party providers go through a 4-6 month QA cycle.  I 
expect a faster ramp for public domain software.

I hope this helps. 
 
