	Hey, I stand by it!! I really don't think it's possible to "get
UNIX right" or to get any operating system of nontrivial size "right"
anymore. Companies that are in the computing dark-ages mentality, where
the operating system is never wrong and you can call the vendor and beg
and scrape and plead for a fix (if they ever acknowledge there is a
problem) are the ones who are failing in the UNIX arena. It's interesting
to see that for all of AIX' problems, IBM is at least being aggressive
about fixing bugs. (The fixes are sometimes weirdly AIXish, but that's
another story)

	UNIX isn't VMS. One of UNIX's real disadvantages is that it's
impossible for any vendor to be "the only game in town" like one could
with VMS or MVS. That means that the customer gets to choose, and that
means some customers may evaluate things like ready availability of
patches as high priorities. A recent example is the CERT advisory
about HP/UX's yellow pages security hole. A systems administrator
who cares to can get the fix *RIGHT* *NOW*. Not next week. That is a
good thing. Digital needs to realize this.

