In article <1993Jan15.194312.3438@sol.UVic.CA>, zapantis@uvphys.phys.uvic.ca (Nik Zapantis) writes:
> Does anyone know or care to comment (DEC?) as to why a 64-bit VMS is not
> a priority? Is it only the address space that is not 64-bits, or the entire
> OS?

It is chiefly the address space.  In a way you could say it is the entire OS.
For eaxmple the various longword counters in the system data structures have
not become quadwords.  This is not a problem because there are very few cases
where VMS needs to count higher than about four billion.  (The one exception
I can think of right off is the quadword binary time format... but VMS has
always kept time in this format.)

> Does anyone know what kind of a performance penalty we'll pay for not
> running a (full?) 64-bit OS in a number crunching environment?

Unless you need a 64-bit virtual address space, none that I can see.  You will
be able to have quadword-sized integer and floating point data in your programs
and the compilers will generate code so that the Alpha chip will work on this
data with its "native" quadword operations.  The fact that no (or very few) VMS
system services have had their arguments extended to quadwords shouldn't bother
you in the slightest.  (You don't really need 64-bit event flag numbers, 64-bit
I/O function codes, 64-bit completion status, etc., do you?)

