Subj:   Abstracts for DECUS U

Saturday/Sunday
March 27-28
VMS Crash Dump Analysis - A Crash Course (Two Day)
 
This seminar provides insight as to how a system crash occurs, but 
more importantly, how you arrived at the situation that caused the 
system to crash. It includes in-depth analysis of a sample crash 
including the use of MAP files, LISTING files, as well as a full 
understanding of the VAX instructions that alter the flow of the code. 

This is extremely important since it is quite simple to decode an 
ACCESS VIOLATION (the most common cause of BUGCHECKS), but the problem 
that caused that violation to occur may very likely not be in the 
immediate vicinity of the failing PC.  It probably was the result of 
previous routines executed prior to the failing code that actually set 
up the circumstances for the signaled failure. 

Topics covered include:
~ Description of a BUGCHECK
~ The Data That is Captured When a BUGCHECK Occurs
~ Description of the STACK POINTER
~ The Effect of the JSB/BSBx and RSB Instructions on the STACK
~ The Effect of the CALLx and RET Instructions on the STACK
~ The Need for VMS Listings When Troubleshooting Most System BUGCHECKS
~ Understanding the VMS Modular Executive
~ Understanding How VMS Handles Exceptions (Establishing and Locating 
  Exception Handlers)
~ Template Forms for Collecting Information for the BIG THREE 
  BUGCHECKS (INVEXCEPTN, SSRVEXCEPT, PGFIPLHI)
~ How to Begin Troubleshooting Those BUGCHECKS that are not Included 
  in the BIG THREE
                                                                                
Instructors:   Ruth Goldenberg & Jim Mehlhop

Credentials:                                                                    
Ruth Goldenberg, the principal author of the VMS V4.4 and V5.2
Internals and Data Structures books, has been reading VMS exec code
for 15 years.  Ms. Goldenberg developed and taught Digital employees 
the two-week long courses "VMS System Seminar" and "Troubleshooting 
VMS."  She is a member of VMS Engineering. 

Jim Mehlhop has been working with VAX machines since the prototype 
testing in 1977. Mr. Mehlhop was a charter member of the Crash Dump 
Analysis team at the Digital CSC, and while there, averaged three to 
four analyses a day for approximately nine years.  He has been working 
for the PARSEC Group for the last three years where he teaches a VMS 
Internals course, as well as a week long course in Crash Dump 
Analysis. 

Prerequisites of Attendees: 
System programmers developing system level code or experienced system 
managers benefit from this seminar.  Prior completion of in depth 
courses on VMS Internals and MACRO programming is a MUST. The Digital 
Press Book "VAX/VMS Internals and Data Structures" is referenced 
during the seminar.  Those attendees who own a copy benefit by 
bringing it with them to the seminar. 

L002
Saturday/Sunday
March 27-28
DCL is a Programming Language (Two Day)
  
This seminar teaches participants how to use the Digital Command 
Language (DCL) as a real programming language to accomplish useful 
tasks.  Because DCL is relatively easy to learn and is available on 
all VMS systems, it opens up the power of programming to people who 
otherwise might not have access to conventional programming languages.  
This seminar is intended for system managers, operators, programmers, 
and anyone else who needs to become proficient using DCL.

The primary focus during the first day is on key concepts and 
strategies for structuring DCL applications.  The primary focus during 
the second day is on methodology for structuring applications.  More 
sophisticated techniques and example programs are covered on the 
second day.

Topics covered on the first day include:
~ Basic DCL Facilities
  ~~ Symbols, data, and expressions
  ~~ Substitution
  ~~ Defining keys
  ~~ Command line evaluation
  ~~ Lexical functions
  ~~ Flow of control
  ~~ Logical names
~ Specific Uses of DCL
  ~~ Displaying and directing output

Topics covered on the second day include:
~ Specific Uses of DCL
  ~~ Obtaining user input
  ~~ Devices, files and directories
~ The Structure of an Application
  ~~ DCL environment
  ~~ Error handling
  ~~ Interrupt handling
  ~~ Application structure

Instructor:    William Denning

Credentials:                                                                    
William Denning is an independent consultant in Houston, Texas, 
specializing in VAX VMS and ULTRIX training.  Prior to his current 
position, Mr.Denning was the technical systems training coordinator 
with a large engineering firm.  He also spent four years writing and 
supporting DCL installation procedures for third-party software for a 
customer base of 2500 installations.  He holds a bachelor's degree in 
mathematics from the University of Houston.

Prerequisites of Attendees:                                                     
Attendees should have a comfortable familiarity working on VMS 
systems, and should understand basic DCL command syntax.  Six months' 
experience writing small DCL applications is recommended.

L101
Saturday
March 27
Designing Client/Server Distributed Applications  

This seminar gives attendees a thorough overview of the technology 
and techniques required to design and implement network-based 
applications that follow the client/server model.   

The seminar approaches client/server technology from three levels of 
architecture and design:

~ High Level Design Using Database and Transaction Processing
  Platforms and Tools
~ Intermediate Level Design Using Software Message Bus Services
~ Low Level Design Using Remote Procedure Call Interface

At the conclusion of the seminar, attendees are aware of techniques 
to accomplish the following:

~ Select the Appropriate Level of Technology for Their Application
~ Design a Comprehensive Architectural Framework for
  Enterprise-Wide Distributed Applications

Seminar attendees should be capable of devising a high-level design 
for a client/server application, and translating this design into the 
architectural building blocks for subsequent detailed design.

Instructor:    David McGinness

Credentials:                                                                    
David McGinness is the senior database administrator for PacifiCare 
Health Services responsible for a 8.0 Gbyte Rdb/VMS database.  Mr. 
McGinness also assists in the optimization of a large ACMS/Rdb 
application.  Prior to joining PHS, he was a principal software 
specialist in Digital's Software Services Engineering group where he 
supported Digital's database products.  While at Digital, he designed 
special-purpose client/server applications for Digital customers and 
consulted on distributed transaction processing applications using 
general-purpose client/server methodologies. 

Prerequisites of Attendees: 
Attendees should have a good working knowledge of application-level 
program design and coding.  Familiarity with one or more high-level 
programming languages (FORTRAN, BASIC, or C) and exposure to file or
database application programming is assumed.

L102
Saturday
March 27
Programming with VAX SQL
  
Relational databases form one of the most powerful tools of current
computing technologies.  They provide extreme flexibility in data 
retrieval and allow for dynamic changes to data definitions.  One 
language for interacting with relational databases is SQL, which has 
been adopted as a standard by the American National Standards 
Institute (ANSI).  VAX SQL, Digital's implementation of the SQL 
standard, interfaces with Digital's DSRI compliant products, including 
Rdb/VMS and VIDA. 

Topics covered include:
~ Rdb and the VAX Information Architecture
  ~~ Rdb storage engines
  ~~ Relationship between VAX SQL, Rdb/VMS and RDO
~ Manipulating Data with SQL
  ~~ The select statement
  ~~ Predicate definition
  ~~ Projections and joins
  ~~ Union operator
  ~~ Data aggregation
  ~~ Aliases
  ~~ Transaction definition and locking
  ~~ Updating data
  ~~ Cursor statements
  ~~ Accessing multiple databases
~ SQL Program Interface
  ~~ Module language
  ~~ Language support
  ~~ Handling null data
  ~~ Handling exceptions
~ Other SQL Interfaces
 ~~ Dynamic SQL
 ~~ SQL services
                                                                                
Instructors:   Keith Hare & Tim Mueller

Credentials:                                                                    
Keith Hare is a senior consultant with JCC and has been working with
Rdb/VMS since version 1.0.  Mr. Hare's recent experiences have 
included the administration and tuning of a number of large Rdb/VMS 
database applications. 

Tim Mueller, as a consultant, has provided business information 
systems and training for a broad range of clients in international 
service and manufacturing industries.  Presently vice chair of the 
CODASYL Forms Interface Management Committee, Mr. Mueller is a 
technical contributor to the draft standard on which DECforms is 
based.  He is a past member of ACM and IEEE Computer Society, and at 
present he is the chair of the DECUS Data Management Systems SIG.  
Recent projects have been concentrated in the DECforms, ACMS and SQL 
environments. 

Prerequisites of Attendees:                                                     
Attendees should have had exposure to database application design and
some familiarity with relational database concepts.  Experience with
the VMS programming environment and Rdb/VMS are also useful.

L103
Saturday
March 27
A Technical Introduction to TCP/IP
  
Transmission Control Protocol/Internet Protocol (TCP/IP) is currently 
the networking protocol of choice for interconnecting dissimilar 
computers and operating systems.  In the world of engineering and 
scientific workstations, TCP/IP is the standard, forcing many VAX VMS 
installations to begin looking at the problem of integrating VAX VMS 
systems into a TCP/IP network.   

The seminar presents a general overview of the TCP/IP protocol family
including the Internet networking model, protocol layering, error
detection/recovery, network control, addressing, routing, symbolic 
name to address resolution, reliable data streams and unreliable 
datagrams. The seminar then describes many of the standard TCP/IP 
applications and services. Attendees learn how new TCP/IP services can 
be implemented and how to use TCP/IP as a general interprocess/inter-
machine communication facility. 

Topics covered include:
~ TCP/IP Protocol Overview
~ Internet Networking Model
~ Protocol Layering
~ Error Detection/Recovery
~ Network Control
~ Addressing
~ Routing
~ Symbolic Name-to-Address Resolution
~ Reliable Data Streams
~ TCP/IP Standard Applications and Services
~ VAX VMS Implications
~ Overhead
~ Security
~ Functionality
~ TCP/IP Services Implementation

Time permitting, additional topics may be covered, such as:

~ High Performance Network Implementation Techniques for VAX VMS
~ TCP/IP Remote Procedure Call Standard
~ TCP/IP Network File Systems
                                                                                
Instructor:    L. Stuart Vance
                                                                                
Credentials:                                                                    
L. Stuart Vance holds a B.S. degree in Mathematics from the University 
of Texas.  Mr. Vance worked at the University of Texas on the startup 
staffs of both the UT System Center for High Performance Computing and 
the UT System Office of Telecommunications Services.  While working 
for the latter, he served for two years as the manager of the Texas 
Higher Education Network (THEnet).  He left the University in July 
1989 to become the manager of Technical Support of TGV, Inc.

Prerequisites of Attendees:                                                     
Attendees should have a general knowledge of networking.  Some 
rudimentary knowledge of VMS and operating system concepts are also 
beneficial.

L104
Saturday
March 27
Terminal Server Management and LAT Network Concepts
  
This seminar is for system and network managers who are responsible 
for DECservers and Local Area Transport (LAT) network software on VAX 
VMS nodes. Basic concepts explored build a foundation in understanding 
the LAT software, troubleshooting, and network management.

Seminar orientation is towards those who are new to LAT and terminal 
servers, but is also of interest to those with some experience.  The 
new VMS LAT software and the differences between each DECserver 
product are explained to understand how they are managed in the same 
network.

Topics covered include:
~ Introduction to LAT and Terminal Servers
~ DECserver and LAT Software Concepts
~ How DECservers Down-line Load
~ Using Terminal Server Manager (TSM)
~ LAT Network Management Issues
~ LAT Load Balancing
~ LAT Group Codes
~ Basic Port and Load Host Configuration
~ LAT Printer Configuration and Problem Solving
~ Basic LAT and DECserver Troubleshooting
                                                                                
Instructor:    Mike Metzler
                                                                                
Credentials:                                                                    
Mike Metzler is a senior network specialist at Digital's Customer 
Support Center in Colorado Springs.  Mr. Metzler specializes in 
supporting the DECserver product line, VMS/LAT, and TCP/IP Ethernet 
Communication Servers. He has been working with Digital products for 
more than 10 years and has presented his LAT Seminar on DECserver 
products more than 40 times for DECUS.

Prerequisites of Attendees:                                                     
Attendees should be familiar with basic systems management of a VAX 
VMS system or other LAT host, or interested and involved with the
configuration and management of terminal servers. 

L105
Saturday
March 27
Introduction to PATHWORKS
  
This seminar is a basic introduction to the terms, concepts and
architecture of Digital's PATHWORKS product.  The seminar provides a 
good foundation for individuals who are about to install a PATHWORKS 
network for the first time.  For those who recently installed 
PATHWORKS, it provides an opportunity for clarifying some of your 
unanswered questions about the system and how it works.  To get the 
most benefit from a PATHWORKS based network, it is essential to have a 
broad understanding of its features and capabilities.  This seminar 
gets the new PATHWORKS user started along the right path and clears up 
some of the potentially confusing concepts associated with PATHWORKS. 
 
Topics covered include:
~ Introduction to PATHWORKS Components
~ DECnet for DOS and VAX
~ Installing PATHWORKS for VMS and PATHWORKS for DOS
~ PATHWORKS Utilities and Tools
~ Remote File Access
~ Network Print Services
~ Security Considerations
~ PATHWORKS System Administration
~ Ethernet Adapters
~ Office Ethernet Cabling

Instructor:    Gary Mauler
                                                                                
Credentials:                                                                    
Gary Mauler is a senior engineer with Westinghouse Electric 
Corporation. Mr. Mauler has been involved with PC networking since 
1982.  His experience includes Ethernet local area networks as well as 
wide area network configurations.  He is currently involved in the 
systems design and administration of a large corporate network 
consisting of various manufacturers' PCs connected to many types of 
VAX server configurations. 

Prerequisites of Attendees:                                                     
A general background of PCs and VAXes would be helpful but is not 
strictly required.

L106
Saturday
March 27
PATHWORKing Microsoft Windows
  
This seminar is directed at system managers, administrators and users 
who want to understand and make the most of Microsoft Windows.  
Emphasis is on the use of PATHWORKS to intergrate and manage personal 
computers running Microsoft's windowing interface.  An overview of 
MS-DOS as it relates to the MS-Windows operating environment is 
included.  DOS 5.0 and Windows 3.1 topics such as memory basics, 
environment variables, and Terminate and Say Resident (TSR) programs 
are reviewed.

The seminar then focuses on Windows, filling in the gaps between 
Microsoft's (SETUP) and Digital's (WIN2SETU) idea of installing and 
administering Windows (local verses network), management of INI files, 
Windows applications (installation and management), and tricks to 
simplify Windows networking.  These topics are viewed from a 
management perspective and offer solutions for maximizing flexibility, 
simplifying the users' interaction, and minimizing Windows management 
headaches.  Insight into the emerging New Technology (NT) operating 
system is also provided.

Topics covered include:
~ Why MS-Windows?
~ What it Takes
  ~~ Hardware
  ~~ Software
~ Configuring PATHWORKS for DOS Clients
~ Windows Configuration Options: Local vs. Remote
~ Installation
~ Advantages and Disadvantages of Windows' Modes
~ Tuning Windows
~ User / Workstation Independence
~ Dealing with INI Files
~ Diskless Workstations
~ DOS Applications under Windows
  ~~ Program manager
  ~~ Saber menu
  ~~ Windows workstation
                                                                                
Instructors:   Brad Cooper

Credentials:                                                                    
Brad Cooper manages network services and is an instructor at Millsaps 
College in Jackson, Mississippi.  As manager of Network Services, Mr. 
Cooper oversees operations that provide network support to users of 
VMS, ULTRIX, OS/2, DOS and Macintosh systems.  He has a masters and 
bachelors in business administration with an emphasis in computing.

Prerequisites of Attendees:                                                     
Experience administrating a PATHWORKS network and using both MS-DOS 
and Windows is assumed.

L107
Saturday
March 27
DECnet VAX Security
  
Along with the flexibility of DECnet in accommodating the complex
interactions of today's extended networks come the problems of 
ensuring that network operations are as secure as possible against the 
hackers, worms, and viruses that have recently been plaguing all 
facets of the computing industry.  Securely connecting to a network 
has become an issue of major concern.  This seminar presents 
information on how to configure DECnet in a manner that provides 
maximum protection while still allowing unhindered functionality.  We 
also discuss audit trail, incident handling, and prosecution issues. 

Topics covered include:
~ Introduction to DECnet - Terminology and Functionality
~ The DECnet Databases 
~ Default Configurations
~ Network Threats and Inherent Vulnerabilities
~ Object-level Security Methods
~ Documented, Little Documented and Undocumented Features
~ FAL Logging, NETSERVER and Other Auditing Techniques
~ Additional Security and Audit-trail Examples and Methods

Case studies of recently publicized network abuses (worms) are also
planned. 

Instructor:    Ken A.L. Coar
                                                                                
Credentials:                                                                    
Ken A. L. Coar is a principal information systems specialist with 
Digital Equipment Corporation.  Mr. Coar has been working with the VAX 
VMS operating system for more than 13 years, since prior to version 
1.0, and has over 10 years of experience with DECnet-VAX software and 
applications, VMS system management, system programming, the ALL-IN-1 
product, and computer security.  He has been active in the DECUS U.S. 
Chapter, both at the local and the national level since 1983, and is 
currently an advisor to the VMS Systems SIG Steering Committee and a 
DEC counterpart to the Security SIG.

Prerequisites of Attendees:                                                     
VAX VMS system managers who are responsible for networked systems 
as well as others interested in DECnet-VAX security benefit from 
this seminar.  Basic knowledge of VMS is assumed.  Additional
background in system or network management or similar technical
experience is helpful but is not required. 

L108
Saturday
March 27
VAX VMS Performance Management Version 5, the Next Page
  
Performance management requires an effective understanding of the
operation of VAX VMS.  Increased performance is dependent on
understanding how VAX VMS utilizes the available resources.  With
advances in technology the cost of CPU horsepower is rapidly 
declining, but these same advances also lead to applications and work 
methodologies that require increasingly more resources.  The million 
instruction per second (mips) computer that serviced the needs of 20 
users yesterday is unable to effectively support even one user today. 

This seminar covers the underlying concepts of VAX VMS within the
context of performance management.  By understanding these concepts, 
you are able to provide maximum machine performance without 
deteriorating the user's productivity. 

Topics covered include:
~ Virtual Memory Subsystem
~ I/O Subsystem
~ Performance Monitoring
~ Performance Analysis
~ Tuning Procedures
~ AI Tuning Procedures
~ File System Design
~ File System Monitoring
~ File System Tuning
~ Performance Programming 

Instructor:    James Coburn
                                                                                
Credentials:                                                                    
James Coburn is a VAX systems manager with 13 years experience in
systems management, software development, and users consulting with 11 
of his 13 years of experience on VAX computers.  Mr. Coburn has worked 
with a majority of the VAX CPUs since the introduction of the original
VAX-11/780 running VMS Version 1.0. 

Prerequisites of Attendees:                                                     
Attendees interested in getting the most performance from their VAXes
benefit from this seminar.  A background in systems management is 
useful since this seminar is not recommended for "raw" novices.  
Intermediate and advanced system persons benefit the most from this 
seminar.  Previous knowledge of VMS internals is NOT required.

L201
Sunday
March 28
Introduction to ALL-IN-1 Programming
  
This seminar introduces the student to the concepts and techniques 
needed to design, code, test, implement, manage and maintain useful 
ALL-IN-1 business applications and customizations. 

The seminar defines the capabilities of the collection of elements 
that comprise the ALL-IN-1 V3.0 application programming toolkit, such 
as display forms, scripts and command procedures, and text processing 
templates.  The basic techniques used to create and manipulate each of 
these elements is demonstrated using simple examples.

Topics covered include:
~ What is ALL-IN-1 Application Programming
  ~~ Concepts of programming
  ~~ Elementary components of an application
  ~~ Evaluating the feasibility of an application
~ Components of the ALL-IN-1 V3.0 Application Programming Environment
 ~~ VAX FMS screen forms and named data
 ~~ Lexical functions
 ~~ DO mode scripts
 ~~ SCRIPT mode scripts
 ~~ COMMAND procedures
 ~~ MERGE templates
 ~~ Incorporating external applications
 ~~ Tools for application programming

Attention is also given to such issues as user interface standards,
context-sensitive help, performance considerations, and customization
management capabilities. 
                                                                                
Instructor:    Frank Nicodem
                                                                                
Credentials:                                                                    
Frank Nicodem is a software consultant for Digital at its Enterprise
Integration Center, in Charlotte, North Carolina.  Mr. Nicodem has 
been involved with ALL-IN-1 for more than 10 years and is currently 
working in the Cross-Industries Solutions group. 

Prerequisites of Attendees:                                                     
It is recommended that the attendee have a working knowledge of
ALL-IN-1 at the user level, and some familiarity with at least one of
the major ALL-IN-1 subsystems, such as Electronic Messaging or Word
Processing. 

L202
Sunday
March 28
An Introduction to X Windows Programming
  
"User-friendly", "intuitive", "easy-to-use", these are some of the 
ways that graphic user interfaces have been described.  They are all 
these things, but that ease of use comes at a price.  The developer 
must build in all the "convenience".  In fact, the approach to 
programming for "point and click" systems is radically different from 
"traditional" programming.  To aid in developing applications with 
graphic interfaces, several interface systems have been developed, 
most notably X Windows.  This seminar presents the rudiments of 
programming applications with a graphical user interface: the central 
concept of event-driven programming, with examples showing the 
"how-to" of creating windows, painting the contents, modifying the 
contents based on user input, scrolling, dialog boxes, and pop-up 
windows.  These example use the Xlib.h and Xutil.h routines.

Topics covered include:
~ Xlib and Xutil Routines
~ Painting the Screen
~ Drawing in X Windows
~ Handling Text
~ Event-Driver Programming
~ Programming for the Mouse and the Keyboard
~ "Rubber-Band" Lines
~ Scrolling
~ Multiple Windows
~ Pop-up Windows and Dialog Boxes
                                                                                
Instructor:    Dan Jinguji
                                                                                
Credentials:                                                                    
Dan Jinguji is a software instructor/consultant for the Boeing 
Company.  Mr. Jinguji is the graphic interface instructor for the 
Boeing Defense and Space group and one of their primary software 
engineering instructors.  He has 15 years of experience in teaching 
technical material. He has an M.S. in computer science from the 
University of Washington. 

Prerequisites of Attendees:                                                     
Familiarity with Windows (as a user) is absolutely required.  
High-order language programming experience is also required.  The 
attendee should be competent in C programming. 

L203
Sunday
March 28
Advanced LAT Concepts
  
This is an in-depth, technical seminar about the Local Area Transport 
(LAT) protocol oriented towards system and network managers that have 
a basic understanding of Local Area Networks (LAN) but need a better 
understanding of the LAT protocol.  This seminar begins by guiding the 
attendees through a history of why Digital Equipment Corporation 
developed the LAT protocol, through its assumptions, and how it works. 

Basic troubleshooting of LAT protocol problems is discussed so that 
faults on the network can be identified and isolated based on an 
understanding of message exchange and synchronization.  This is useful 
to those who need to identify problems on their LAN that are related 
to LAT so that these problems can be corrected.  This seminar gives 
detailed explanations of LAT concepts in an effort to strengthen the 
attendees knowledge of how LAT fits into the networking picture.  It 
helps the network planner take into consideration the LAT protocol 
when a network must be designed and the LAT protocol is included 
in that design.  The information contained in this seminar assists the 
network planner make informed decisions when putting a network in 
place, managing it, and maintaining it.  

The mechanics of the LAT protocol are discussed but due to the 
proprietary nature of LAT and the legal issues surrounding the LAT 
licensing with Digital Equipment Corporation, this seminar does not 
analyze or examine "LAT Architecture Specification" itself.

Topics covered include:
~ LAT History
~ Protocol Assumptions
~ Circuit and Slot Layer Concepts
~ Solicitation Concepts
~ Performance
~ Protocol Violations and Basic Troubleshooting
                                                                                
Instructor:    Michael Raspuzzi
                                                                                
Credentials:                                                                    
Michael Raspuzzi is a senior software engineer at Digital in 
Littleton, Massachusetts.  Mr. Raspuzzi is responsible for the LAT VMS 
software and has authored portions of LAT VMS and the VXT 2000 LAT 
software. He also participated in LAT Architecture enhancements for 
LAT V5.2.  He has been at Digital for eight years and has been working 
with LAT for four years.  Prior to working with LAT, he worked on 
