L204
Sunday
March 28
Bridges, Extended LANs, and Routers
  
This seminar covers internetworking issues.  Approximately equal time 
is  devoted to bridging and routing.  Ethernet and FDDI are the 
primary data links discussed.  General routing concepts, including 
multiprotocol routing are covered.  The concept, architecture, and 
specific Digital products for bridges are discussed.  Positioning 
relative to the OSI network reference model is used to set 
expectations on bridge service.  Specific examples regarding bridging 
between FDDI and Ethernet vs. IEEE 802.3 LANs are explored.  In-class 
exercises on the Spanning Tree operation are provided. 

Traffic management in Extended LANs and wide area Extended LANs are
discussed including filtering, monitoring, and network management. The 
Routing services are also examined, including positioning relative to 
the ISO OSI network reference model, LAN and WAN message forwarding, 
Adaptive Routing, Path Splitting, building and maintaining the Routing 
Table, and Router packet-forwarding performance. 

Topics covered include:
~ LANs and Extended LANs
  ~~ LAN definitions and limitations  
  ~~ Ethernet and mixed data link configurations
~ Bridges
  ~~ Definitions and models
  ~~ Spanning tree algorithm 
  ~~ Address and protocol filtering and management
~ Routers
  ~~ Introduction: basic concepts and terminology
  ~~ Network layer addressing
  ~~ DECnet Phase IV vs. DECnet/OSI Phase V routing
~ Bridges vs. Routers
  ~~ Summary comparison of bridges and routers
                                                                                
