L208
Sunday
March 28
Security Incident Response: What to do When the System's Been 
Compromised

Security incidents happen: cyberpunks probe and penetrate networks and 
systems; vandals pilfer PCs; employees embezzle from computer 
controlled accounts; and jack-hammers cleave (network) cables.  The 
quality of our response to a security incident can directly determine 
how much harm our organization suffers.  Fundamentally, incident 
response deals with those mechanisms which mitigate, correct, or 
recover from the effects of harmful acts and circumstances.  This 
seminar addresses the implementation and operation of an Secuirty 
Incident Response Team (SIRT) to expeditiously deal with these 
factors.  This "team" may consist of one, several, or many people.  
The strengths and weakness of the SIRT approach are examined.  The 
technical and managerial resources needed to support a SIRT's work are 
identified.  System and network managers who need to know what to do 
when "security's been compromised" find this session particularly 
valuable.

Seminar participants understand:
~ The Motives, Methods and Techniques Used to Compromise Systems and
  Networks.
~ The Technical Tools and Resources That can be Used to Mitigate Harm
  From an Ongoing Security Incident.
~ What Resources are Available to Recover From a Catastrophic Security
  Event, and How to Use Them.
~ What Requirements Need to be Met in Order To Pursue any Potential
  Legal Proceedings for Harmful Acts Committed.

Special areas of interest include:
~ TCP/IP and DECnet Based Networks
~ UNIX, ULTRIX, and VMS Features (Including Some Un-, Under- or       
  Poorly-Documented Features)
  ~~ Audit and alarms
  ~~ Process monitoring and control
  ~~ Configuration/operation for improved fault isolation
~ Communications Requirements: Within Team; Throughout Organization;   
  and With Other SIRTs.
~ "Sting" Techniques Such as Traps and Trip-Wires.
~ Investigation Techniques
~ Contingency Planning
~ Using the SIRT's Experiences to Improve Preventative Measurers.

