We had discussions with DEC and they made it clear that they were willing to
sell Alpha at any level - at a price of course. We could license the die and put
our own goodies on-chip, we could by chips, we could buy bare boards, we could
buy complete packaged systems. They also seemed very keen to push third party
specialist shops doing ruggedisation etc and working the specialist markets that
DEC does not. 

The most interesting project I heard was a suggestion to build an Alpha TRAM
using an alpha chip and an inmos T9000 to build a MIMD type system. I don't
think DEC managed to get anyone interested in producing it though. I think that
it might not have been such a great idea. If you take a standard FDDI adaptor
and a routing chip (gigaswitch?) you could do the same stuff with standard parts
probably much cheaper. The main advantage that the T9000 has is having the comms
software in microcode and having a light O/S layer offering low latency links,
an Alpha/T9000 bodge would lose that.

For embedded control DEC could make Alpha very attractive by offering a
transputer like object with fast serial (or parallel!) links to other beasties
on chip. It may be pricy now but in a couple of years mass production should be
feasible.

