In article <1993Jan15.142835.1014@dragon.com>, cts@dragon.com writes...
>I have an application which is being moved from a PDP11/44 (may it rest in
>peace real soon now) to a MicroVAX system (actually, its one of the redundant
>Mira systems).  Anyway, this application used to use a DR11-W on the PDP
>side;  this is being changed to a DRV11-W.  

> Further, as things are set up now, each QIO write requires
>4 write cycles.  The first strobes the FC1 bit high - this tells the
>custom hardware on the other end to prepare itself.  Then, the data
>is transfered.  Then we repeat the previous two steps.  We're waiting
>for an interrupt back from the device for each of these.  (Note, the FC1
>bit is a replacement for the old GO line on the DR11-W, apparently this
>cycle did not exist on the PDP side)
> 

This is probably not necessary and may cause problems.  The typical thing to do
in going from a DR11W to a DRV11W is to sense the change in the state of READY
as a replacement for the GO bit pulse on the user side.  (When GO is set in the
CSR, the READY state changes).  DEC types (I think it was the author of
XADRIVER) have confirmed that this is the apropriate thing to do, you can
actually get into trouble using a function bit, I don't recall the details.

