|>
|>In the Jan 4th. edition of DN&R there is an interview with W.R. Demmer 
|>DEC VP for Alpha and VAX systems. 
|>There (p. 42), he states that there is no schedule for the release of 
|>a 64-bit OpenVMS version and he goes on to state a number of reasons for the
|>delays.
|>Does anyone know or care to comment (DEC?) as to why a 64-bit VMS is not
|>a priority? Is it only the address space that is not 64-bits, or the entire
|>OS?
|>Does anyone know what kind of a performance penalty we'll pay for not 
|>running a (full?) 64-bit OS in a number crunching environment?
|>
|>thanks 
|>

Nik,
	If you look at the Memory Management mechanism employed in the AXP VMS
version you will notice pretty quickly that DEC is already taking advantage
(although in a small way) of the 64bit'ness of the AXP platform. As too why
a full blown 64 bit version of VMS is not a priority I would say one word..
"functionality".. ie, DEC was much more concerned about getting their existing
layered products and (the OS itself) ported to AXP.. before thinking of an
entire re-write of their mother ship operating system. What you want to do
really is getting the black box in place and slowly swap out the components
within it...

I don't believe you will pay much of a penalty in a number crunching environment
for lack of 64bit OS support.. remember the OS is really there to service 
requests from applications.. without getting too specific; in a number 
crunching mode (with little or no I/O) you are not really asking the OS to 
do a lot of stuff for you..

-Larry.


