In article <15947@hq.hq.af.mil>, parsons@postmaster@hq.af.mil (Mike Parsons) writes:
|>I have a requirement to have users access VAXes across a large TCP/IP backbone.
|>The users need to be using DECNET as a transport because of application
|>constraints imposed by the software developer.  The question I have is how or
|>what products can be used to allow either encapsulation or translation of the
|>DECNET protocol going into the TCP/IP backbone and reemerging on the other end
|>and being changed back to DECNET.  The backbone has been known to suffer
|>congestion on ocasion which may also impact timers, etc.
|>
|>Any help would be appreciated.
|>
|>Thanks.

The Wollongong Group products (WIN/TCP and PathWay for VMS) provide this
capability; we call it DNIP for DecNet over IP. I know of at least one
other TCP/IP vendor that offers similar capabilities.

Basically what's required is a DECnet routing node on each end of the
TCP/IP link running TCP/IP and DNIP. DECnet on these nodes see the DNIP
connection as a synchronous serial line to the remote machine.

