> As too why
> a full blown 64 bit version of VMS is not a priority I would say one word..
> "functionality".. ie, DEC was much more concerned about getting their existing
> layered products and (the OS itself) ported to AXP.. before thinking of an
> entire re-write of their mother ship operating system. What you want to do
> really is getting the black box in place and slowly swap out the components
> within it...

If the experience of That Other Computer Manufacturer is any indication, it
will take quite a while for VMS to become 64-bit capable and a much, much
longer time for your average utility to do the same. The situation is indeed
very similar to what happened when IBM introduced the XA architecture,
extending address space from 24 to 31 bits (the current architecture by the way
is called ESA - this is already old history). It didn't take long for them to
come up with an operating system which could make use of the extra real memory
for user virtual pages. It took a while for them to produce a complete
XA-capable system with new calls, different parameter list formats, the ability
to identify which subprograms can't handle the new addressing and must be
loaded below the 16M line and called in 24 bit mode, and so on. We've had a
working XA-capable system for many years now, and applications which only work
in 24-bit mode are still very common. The reason is that it doesn't really buy
you anything for simple utilities to be able to address all these extra
megabytes. I have tons of programs which fit in a couple score kilobytes and
use maybe 512k of work storage, and that's being generous, and it never occured
to me that I might want to dual-path all that code so they can continue to run
on systems which only support the old 24-bit architecture while at the same
time gaining the ability to address gigabytes of memory I will never need. I
suppose in 5 to 10 years the old 24-bit hardware will have completely
disappeared, but I would still need to convert some system calls to use a newer
format which doesn't really buy me anything.

  Eric


