In article <1993Jan13.015901.4885@spcvxb.spc.edu>, terry@spcvxb.spc.edu (Terry Kennedy, Operations Mgr.) writes:
> In article <1993Jan12.074046.1138@cmkrnl.com>, jeh@cmkrnl.com writes:
>> My first impulse is to get a spare DHV11 and hack it up, doubling the baud
>> clock that's going to one of the dual UART chips.  Looks like this can be done
>> just by adding another crystal.  If that works, try doubling it again.  Then
>> setting one of those two ports to 19200 would get an actual speed of 76800.
>> Of course I have no idea whether the UARTs can handle a baud clock that fast. 
> 
>   I'd advise against it on the DHV. The DHV is "run" by a pair of 8031 micro-
> controllers (like in an LK201 - you get the idea). They will wedge up and
> require a power cycle to clear if they lose an interrupt internally (note that
> this isn't VMS interrupts, but the 8031 being interrupted by the UARTs). You
> would be a lot better off starting with a DHV clone with more horsepower or a
> DHQ11.

Of course then I'll be dealing with different UART chips and I just went and
looked up the DHV's !

The UART chips could handle the increased speed but indeed the DHV can't. In
fact, according to the DHV manual, you can program it for 38.4 kbps, and it can
receive at that rate, but the DMA transmit logic on the board can't go that
fast -- if you program it for 38400 individual characters will be emitted at
that speed but there will be gaps in between for a net throughput of 2000
char/sec. 

So hacking the DHV is indeed pointless.  (Not maybe, but definitely!) The idea
might work on other cards, though.  I think I'll order the DHQ technical
manual and see what it has to say.  

